---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';

const directions = [
  {
    title: 'Native language to English',
    body: 'What someone says in their own language, asked in English. Shows what gets lost or changed when their words are pulled into English.',
  },
  {
    title: 'English to native language',
    body: 'An English phrase, such as one from an app or a form, asked in the person’s own language. Shows how English-based design actually lands on the people using it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Native language to regional dialect',
    body: 'The standard version of a language, asked how it changes in a specific region. The same word can mean something different, or sound different, from one place to the next, even within one language.',
  },
];

const glossary = [
  {
    phrase: 'Arre, fees maangenge kya?',
    literal: 'Oh, will they ask for fees?',
    implied: 'Alarm. Money requested inside an official flow reads as a possible scam.',
  },
  {
    phrase: 'Haye, ab phir se sab documents?',
    literal: 'Oh no, all the documents again?',
    implied: 'Fatigue and exasperation at repeated requests. Burden, not confusion.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="Thick translation and the bidirectional validation survey: the core technique behind confirming that a meaning moment is well supported."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About / Methodology</p>
      <h1>Thick translation, checked in both directions.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In India, people rarely mean exactly what the words say. The core technique behind this toolkit is to translate carefully,
        keeping context and feeling along with the words, and then to check the translation with the people it came from.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap body">
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="thick">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="thick">What thick translation is</h2>
        <p>
          A phrase can carry a different meaning depending on the language, the tone, the region, or even a gesture that goes with it.
          Translating word for word loses all of that. Thick translation keeps the context and the feeling along with the words.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the process, it appears as two steps in Module 3. <strong>Thick translation</strong> records the literal meaning, the
          context, and alternative readings. <strong>Reverse thick translation</strong> tests that result with the audience. Between
          them, a local language collaborator reviews the work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="bidi">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="bidi">Bidirectional validation</h2>
        <p>
          Once thick translation is done, a survey confirms that it landed correctly. It runs in two directions. In both, it is
          looking for the gap between what the words literally say and what people actually understand.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--2 dirs">
        <article class="card dir">
          <p class="tag">Direction A</p>
          <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="box">English translation<br /><small>or a given target language</small></span>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="box out">Implied meaning<br /><small>in the participant’s language</small></span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Participants are shown the English translation (or the language being translated into) and give the implied meaning
            that they hold in their own language.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card dir">
          <p class="tag">Direction B: the reverse</p>
          <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="box">Phrase in the participant’s language</span>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="box out">Meaning<br /><small>in English</small></span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Participants are shown a phrase in their own language and give what it means in English.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="prose after">
        <p>
          Together these are the technique for confirming that a meaning moment is well supported. When both directions agree, and
          the four <a href={url('/tools/fidelity-protocol/')}>Fidelity Protocol</a> tags are recorded, the moment can pass the
          decision “is the meaning well supported?” and enter the{' '}
          <a href={url('/tools/expression-library/')}>Expression Library</a>. When they do not agree, the finding loops back to
          thick translation for another look.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="directions">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="directions">Three directions of comparison</h2>
        <p>The survey compares meaning along these three directions. They are separate from the three lenses of the Language Lens Audit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        {
          directions.map((d, i) => (
            <article class="card">
              <p class="tag">{i + 1}</p>
              <h3>{d.title}</h3>
              <p>{d.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="signals">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="signals">Small signals count</h2>
        <p>
          The survey also captures the small signals that ordinary surveys ignore but that carry real meaning in an Indian setting:
          exclamations such as “arre” (अरे) or “haye”, the tone someone uses, and gestures. These belong on the Meaning Card next to
          the exact words, not in a footnote.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="glossary">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="glossary">The end product: a linguistic glossary</h2>
        <p>
          Everything from the survey goes into a detailed glossary. For each phrase, two things are recorded: the{' '}
          <strong>literal meaning</strong> (the plain translation of what was said) and the <strong>implied meaning</strong> (the
          equivalent that captures what the person really meant, including tone and feeling).
        </p>
        <p>
          The gap between the two is the useful part. It shows designers exactly where something can look clear on screen but mean
          something else to the person reading it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Illustrative glossary entries (examples, not real participant data)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Phrase</th>
              <th scope="col">Literal meaning</th>
              <th scope="col">Implied meaning</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              glossary.map((g) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" lang="hi-Latn">{g.phrase}</th>
                  <td>{g.literal}</td>
                  <td>{g.implied}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="start">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="start">Before you start</h2>
        <p>Using the toolkit starts with team composition, not process.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>A lead researcher</h3>
          <p>Owns the research question and the process, and runs the primary interviews.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>A local language collaborator</h3>
          <p>An interpreter who is not the same person doing the primary interviewing.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>A second independent reader</h3>
          <p>
            Wherever possible, someone who can confirm or contest a translation before it is trusted. One person’s read, however
            fluent, is still one person’s read.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="prose after">
        <p>
          Before any fieldwork starts, name the research question, secure informed consent, and agree on data deletion rules. This
          step is not optional. Skipping it does not save time, it moves the cost downstream, usually into a re-check that could have
          been avoided.
        </p>
        <p>
          Budget for at least one full loop before expecting stable, library-ready patterns, and treat both libraries as living
          references that keep growing for as long as the product keeps shipping to real people.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .body {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .section:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
  }
  .tag {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .after {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .flow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .box {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .box small {
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .box.out {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .arrow {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    caption-side: bottom;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 12rem;
  }
  thead th {
    background: var(--surface-2);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
